{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nLast spring, I finally made the trip to Japan that I had dreamed of for years. Landing in Tokyo was a sensory overload of neon lights, bustling crowds, and the incredible aroma of street food wafting from every corner. But my heart was set on the ancient capital of Kyoto.\n\nTaking the Shinkansen felt like stepping into the future, slicing through the countryside at breathtaking speeds. Upon arriving in Kyoto, the pace slowed dramatically. I spent my mornings wandering through the Fushimi Inari Shrine, walking beneath thousands of vibrant orange torii gates that seemed to stretch endlessly up the mountain. The silence there, broken only by the rustling of leaves and distant temple bells, was profoundly peaceful.\n\nThe highlight, however, was the cherry blossom season. Walking along the Philosopher's Path, pink petals drifted down like snow, landing gently on the canal below. Locals gathered for *hanami* picnics under the trees, sharing laughter and sake. It was a beautiful reminder to appreciate the fleeting nature of life.\n\nOne evening, I got lost in the narrow alleyways of Gion. By chance, I caught a glimpse of a Geisha hurrying to an appointment, her kimono a blur of silk against the wooden machiya houses. It felt like stepping back in time.\n\nJapan is a country of striking contrasts, where ultra-modern technology coexists seamlessly with deep-rooted traditions. From the quiet reverence of a tea ceremony to the electric energy of Akihabara, every moment was unforgettable. I left with a full camera roll, a lighter heart, and an immediate desire to return. If you haven't visited yet, put it at the top of your list. You won't regret it.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.051065892}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.13473757}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -0.7864187}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -2.12